  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1122 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThe flow aloft becomes much more amplified Wednesday morning, as the \nlarge upper high is moves east towards the Ohio Valley, while an \nupper trof, with multiple large upper waves passing through it \nbuilds into the central Rockies states. The first of several waves \nis forecast to pass well north of Iowa by all available \nguidance, resulting in a dry Wednesday through Thursday, with \nthe main impact being warmer temperatures and breezy conditions.\nWinds Wednesday will be roughly 10 mph higher in western \ncounties than in the east, with a broad forecast range of 15 to \n20 mph south winds, gusting to 20 to 30 mph expected. Fire \ndanger forecasts using GFDI show a bit of \"high\" values in our \nnorthwestern counties of the CWA, but this is worth keeping an \neye on for that day, and also Friday. Unharvested agricultural \nfields may have a somewhat higher threat with a bit more \ncoverage of GFDI AG \"high\". \n\nThe upper trof then will reload for late week, continuing the \nstretch of southerly winds and warm temperatures Thursday through \nSaturday. The NBM continues to show a good potential for mid, and \npossibly upper 60s for each of these days!  That said, there will be \nincreased cloud cover for Saturday, as the front approaches, along \nwith rain chances in the 30 to 60% range. The QPF from this system \ncontinues to show potential of less than 0.25 in all areas, as it is \nexpected to be starved for moisture, with a ridge of high pressure \nfrom the lower Appalachians through Oklahoma cutting off any \ngulf moisture. \n\nFollowing the warm breezy days late this week, will be a seasonal \ncold front arriving  Saturday night or early Sunday, with highs \nlimited to the 40s behind it. (which is far closer to normal than \nmid to upper 60s!).",
